<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pushyamitra Shunga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 185–149 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pushyamitra Shunga was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga dynasty</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Commander of the Maurya army, he assassinated the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, around 185 BCE and took the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. He performed Vedic horse sacrifices, marking a revival of Brahmanical kingship, and Buddhist texts accuse him of persecution, though Buddhist art flourished in his dynasty's time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga.jpg" alt="Pushyamitra Shunga" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leiden University Library / Royal Netherlands Institute of Southeast Asian and Caribbean Studies · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bharhut_Sunga_individual.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Pushyamitra Shunga, also written Pushpamitra, was the founder and first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a>, which he established to succeed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. He took power after assassinating the last Mauryan emperor, Brihadratha. He is said to have driven out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in a Shunga-Greek war and to have ruled for 36 years. The Buddhist Ashokavadana names him as the last Mauryan emperor, which appears to confuse him with Brihadratha. Inscriptions of the Shungas have been found as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, and the Divyavadana says his realm reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>, now Sialkot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><p>He performed the Vedic Ashvamedha sacrifice to legitimise his rule, as the Dhanadeva-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> inscription records. Michael Witzel states that the Manu Smriti, which stresses orthodox faith in statecraft, was first compiled under his rule, and Kaushik Roy sees it as a Brahmanical reaction to the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>. His history is also recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Banabhatta</a>'s Harshacharita.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Buddhist texts accuse Pushyamitra of persecuting Buddhists. The earliest, the Ashokavadana of the 4th or 5th century CE, says his advisers told him he could rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s fame by destroying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He tried to destroy the Kukkutarama monastery, then offered a hundred coins for every monk's head at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>, and was killed by a Buddhist yaksha. Later texts, among them the Vibhasa, the Arya-Manjushri-Mula-Kalpa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranatha">Taranatha</a>'s history of the 16th century, tell similar stories with different places and deaths. Many historians regard them as exaggerated.</p><p>Scholars are divided. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a> found evidence of damage to Buddhist sites at Takshashila and thought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a> stupa vandalised in the 2nd century BCE, and others date damage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kaushambi</a> and Deur Kothar to his time. H. Bhattacharya suggested political motives, since Buddhists may have favoured his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> rivals. Others reject the claim. Etienne Lamotte notes that the legends disagree on where he campaigned and died, and that the dinara named in the Ashokavadana was not in general use before the 1st century BCE. Romila Thapar and Eric Seldeslachts find no firm evidence, and the Buddhist monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> and Sanchi were improved under Shunga rule, though N. N. Ghosh dates Bharhut to later Shunga kings. Pushyamitra was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra">Agnimitra</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pushyamitra Shunga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Demetrius I is generally considered the Greco-Bactrian king who first launched the Greek expansion into India, around 200 BCE, and so the founder of the Indo-Greek realm; the overthrow of the Mauryas by <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b> about 185 BCE is thought to have encouraged it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, was assassinated by his commander-in-chief, <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b>, who founded the Shunga dynasty in the Gangetic heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…basis of the Ashokavadana it is presumed that the stupa may have been vandalised in the 2nd century BCE, an event some have related to the rise of the Shunga ruler <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b>, and it has been suggested that Pushyamitra destroyed it and his son Agnimitra rebuilt it.</blockquote><a href="/work/sanchi">Sanchi · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE the Brahmin general <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b> killed the last Maurya king and founded his own dynasty, and the Gupta records never name the family’s varna.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b>, commander of the Maurya army, assassinated the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, during a military parade and took the throne at Pataliputra.</blockquote><a href="/state/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a></li></ul></section>
